  that are mining that information  but it begs the question how much is  enough and and have we really discovered  all that there is to know about cancer  cells and can we just move forward into  other lines of inquiry and i put this  slide in here to highlight the fact that  there's still a huge amount to discover  this is a paper that was published  earlier this year  lawrence at all in nature and they asked  the question of those  uh of those data sets that are available  for thousands of different cancers  across dozens of different tumor types  currently available out of tcga  data  what additional information is being  identified with each incremental sample  that's analyzed  and so as we look at the graph here  going along the x-axis  starting at zero and going up to close  to five thousand different tumors  we see that the number of new variants  new genes being identified at a mutation  frequency for example at twenty percent  the red line it's kind of leveling out  and so that's indicative of the fact  that we've really found as a community  the genes that are mutated most often in  cancers  20 and above we've identified those  genes  if we change and look at genes that are  mutated less frequently so let's say  three to five percent looking at the  light blue line you'll see that that's  increasing and no sign of leveling off  and this is suggestive of a huge amount  of additional discoveries that need to  be made that are in the lower frequency  range meaning those genes are not not  mutated in a high fraction of patients  or a high fraction of tumors but they  are significant  in a non-trivial fraction of the overall  cancer population  and so the conclusions of this paper  were while  highly mutated genes  genes are mutated at high frequency have  for the most part been found uh based on  this analysis intermediate frequency  down to about two percent for example  will require huge numbers of samples to  complete this catalog to really  understand what the full spectrum of  mutation of mutations are within what we  call quote unquote cancer which is as we  all know an amalgamation of many many  diverse different diseases  their estimation would be that you need  about 2 000  tumors from each of about 50 different  tumor types to  really map this out down to about two  percent frequency and this corresponds  to about an  a total of a hundred thousand different  tumors so as these projects continue on  they'll clearly be making more and more  discoveries um and adding to this body  of work now i should point out that this  is just looking at dna variation it's  not even touching the role of rna or  epigenetics and those are additional  very important lines of inquiry that are  going on by tcga icgc as well as many  other groups around the world  i do want to highlight though that you  know one of the  one of the comments that i often get and  that many of us often get is well you  know what's the importance of a two  percent a gene that's mutated in two  percent of patients what what role is  that really going to do for anybody but  i do want to highlight that crisotinib  which is a really successful  personalized therapy  is targeting out translocations which  are only present in about three to seven  percent of lung cancer patients  so you know not high on the mutation  scale those those alk translocations but  a clear success story for um the  development of personalized therapies  and and exemplified by the story that  we've seen with chronzotinib so i would  argue that going down to two percent um  and understanding the genes that are  mutated at these intermediate  frequencies is an important endeavor for  the the world community to continue  so in summary for the discovery work on  tumor biology there's been over 120  significant mutated genes have been  discovered by the tcga group  and there's been over 550 genes and 120  different fusion genes identified in the  cosmic database so  while we've been able 