ubset of patients and  then that biomarker becomes the  companion diagnostic that's the paradigm  for how it works today  the challenge is um that thus far in the  united states has only been 12 companion  diagnostic tests approved for  personalized therapies in cancer  but the pipeline is huge and so we're  looking at somewhere over 500 depending  on who you ask personalized therapeutics  and development today and so if we take  the complexity of the pharmacopoeia  that's coming down the pipe in addition  to the complexity of cancer that's  emerged from the recent studies that we  just walked through you can see sort of  the factorial combination of all those  different variants plus drug  combinations etc becomes an intractable  problem again when you're dealing with  limited time money and available tissue  uh certainly not extensible in not not  um  uh not  not achievable not explorable given the  current paradigm of testing  so one of the things that illumina is  working to try to support is changing  that paradigm to start with the patient  patient-centered oncology therapeutics  where the patient starts at the center  the next step is then understand the  biology of that patient's tumor really  understand the mutations that that tumor  is carrying and then use that  information for for informed drug  selection um either  and and use that um to really pair the  patient to the right drug at the right  time saving valuable time money um and  labor for  the patient and the doctor  so changing the paradigm instead of  having this sequential one-off testing  the future the future that we're trying  to develop tools for looks something  similar to this where there's a cancer  biopsy the this universal oncology test  is then applied to that cancer biopsy  and there's either an actual variant uh  with an approved informed therapy  available or if there's no actionable  variant detected potentially  looking at additional variants and then  enrolling that patient into a