ers can be  done in a much more streamlined and  efficient way  decentralization to allow genomic  testing uh via standardization and the  streamlined aspects of this program  decentralizing it closer to where  patients are actually treated  it also allows for a mechanism for  investigation and collaboration amongst  pharma cancer centers ourselves as  technology developers and the other  members of this ecosystem so that these  discoveries can move very very quickly  from  just a finding through to something that  can be brought to bear on many patients  across the world  and now switching to our third topic  this is molecular monitoring and this is  basically using deep sequencing to  detect and monitor cancer  what we're going to talk about today is  the deep sequencing of cell free dna  many of you may be familiar with the  huge change in trisomy testing bringing  nipt on board looking for  fetal dna within the maternal dna  bloodstream  very similar concept what we can do in  cancer as well sequencing the fragments  of dna that are floating around in a  patient's  bloodstream and being able to use that  information to detect indicative somatic  variation  pardon me  so one of the quick one of the important  questions is can we detect cancer  earlier  and so we hinted that at the beginning  of this program that despite population  level screening programs we're still as  a community detecting many many cancers  at late stage which limits the treatment  options available for those those  particular individuals and this is a  statistic from a recent report from the  cdc that almost half of the colorectal  cancer  and cervical cancers and a third of the  breast cancer cases in the united states  are diagnosed at late stages late stages  meaning that cancer has metastasized and  spread to other parts of the body  and why well there's a number of reasons  for this  one is lack of compliance for example  or lack of access based on where  patients live or the the healt