e presence  of tumor somatic mutations  in the dna at a high frequency and so  this would suggest the extensibility of  these approaches to earlier stage  patients it's a it's a very important uh  proof of principles that you suggest um  the true promise of this approach going  forward and certainly this will be  followed on by larger uh studies that  that um look deeper and earlier into the  plasma of various patients  another study that also came out of the  johns hopkins group and this is an older  one from a few years ago but i think a  really nice one that that highlights uh  what what is possible and sort of the  types of signals that one can identify  from plasma dna  in this one lyria all used whole genome  sequencing of plasma samples to develop  a plasma aneuploidy score  and they then applied this uh to  uh to  a series of normal individuals as well  as a series series of late stage  metastatic breast and colorectal cancer  patients  interestingly the range of uh tumor  fraction within the blood across these  very late stage patients was highly  variable ranging from about 1.4 to about  50 percent of the the floating uh plasma  dna  excuse me dna in those samples  but i think the graphic from this paper  is is extremely informative and  highlights the power of this approach  you can see in the black  bars that are on the left-hand side of  the graph  those are the signals of this  quote-unquote aneuploidy score from the  normal samples included in the study and  you can see that they are  nearly flat across the board  this is in contrast to the metastatic  patients the colorectal cancer patients  and the breast cancer patients which  across the board  have signals off the charts if you will  here but a clear clear separation  between what is normal in terms of  aneuploidy in the bloodstream versus  what is  indicative of cancer in this particular  study  a limited proof of principle studies a  study only looking at 20 patients but i  think a really nice and clear signal of  the power and the potential of this  approach going forward  another aspect another another  application for deep sequencing of  plasma dna is monitoring cancer in real  time  so in this study researchers are  investigating the sequencing of cell  free dna from plasma for monitoring as  well this is newman at all published  earlier this year in nature medicine um  and they use targeted sequencing of  plasma samples to monitor tumor burden  during treatment what's really  interesting here is you can see um how  they they compare the signal of somatic  variation in the bloodstream  which is the red line in both of these  graphs here to  alternative methods for monitoring the  effectiveness of treatment in this case  the blue  triangles are are referring to  the volume as established by imaging  methods  and so in in the first panel in uh panel  a you can see that the molecular  signature from the plasma dna drops to  zero and is correlated  roughly with what they're seeing from  the tumor volume uh data as well  um  similarly in b so you can see very close  correlation between um the the amount of  circulating tumor dna in the blood and  what um the the the volumetric analysis  of the tumor via imaging is delivering  to the to the uh  the physician um at at the same time so  mutation information from sequencing of  plasma dna correlates well with tumor  volume in this particular uh uh  in this particular data set  what's perhaps more interesting is when  they disagree  and so researchers are investigating  whether or not  we can use this information as an  additional line of evidence  in collab in in conjunction with  imaging information and sometimes they  don't agree so newman at all looked at  these other patients where um in the  first panel in panel e you can see that  their  molecular signature drops to zero um at  the at the time point uh marked number  four uh whereas the tumor volume stays  relatively high and so if all one had to  go on w